        {
          "label": "SHAPES",
          "text": "Squares and triangles lead, drawn from the logomark\u2019s \u201craw block\u201d motif. Use them as a flexible grid of shapes that can be combined, rotated, and overlapped. A circle appears as a minor third shape \u2014 an accent for indicators, bullets, and toggles \u2014 never as a container or part of the logo."
        },
        {
          "label": "LINES",
          "text": "Bold 2\u20133 px Charcoal or Orange rules act as strong section dividers, not thin hairlines. This brand reads heavier and more graphic than a traditional consultancy."
        },
        {
          "label": "GRIDS",
          "text": "An asymmetric modular grid rather than a strict 12-column system \u2014 intentional off-balance compositions that still feel controlled. Build on one base module, let elements break the grid on one side only, and keep margins consistent so the imbalance reads as deliberate.\u2020"
        },
        {
          "label": "PATTERNS",
          "text": "Two textures. The zigzag block pattern \u2014 interlocking Orange and Charcoal chevrons \u2014 for cards, badges, and packaging. The fluted gradient \u2014 vertical ribs of Orange fading to Charcoal, with a dark ribbon form \u2014 for covers, section dividers, and large-format backdrops. Never run text directly over either; set it on a solid block."
        },
        {
          "label": "CONTAINERS",
          "text": "Sharp or slightly clipped-corner blocks, with one corner cut at 45\u00b0 to echo the logomark\u2019s diagonal. Rounded corners are not part of the container language; full-round pills are for interface controls only, such as toggles, sliders, and tags.\u2020"
        },
        {
          "label": "DIVIDERS",
          "text": "Bold, solid-colour rules, occasionally offset from the margin for visual tension. Lines set the weight (2\u20133 px); dividers set the placement \u2014 full-width to separate sections, offset to create tension, never decorative."
        }
